ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE SPECIAL ACT ON ASCERTAINING THE TRUTH OF THE APRIL 16 SEWOL FERRY DISASTER AND BUILDING A SAFER SOCIETY

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Special Act on Ascertaining the Truth of the April 16 Sewol Ferry Disaster and Building a Safer Society and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Authorized Number of Staff of the Commission)
① The authorized number of staff of the Special Investigation Commission on the April 16 Sewol Ferry Disaster under Article 3 of the Special Act on Ascertaining the Truth of the April 16 Sewol Ferry Disaster and Building a Safer Society (hereinafter referred to as the "Act"; and the Commission as the "Commission") shall be 120 persons, including its chairperson, vice-chairperson, and standing commissioners; the authorized numbers by grade shall be as specified in attached Table 1.
② Notwithstanding paragraph ①, until six months after this Decree enters into force, the authorized number of staff of the Commission shall be 90 persons, including its chairperson, vice-chairperson, and standing commissioners; the authorized numbers by grade shall be as specified in attached Table 2.

Article 3 (Organization of the Commission)
① The Secretariat established within the Commission (hereinafter referred to as the "Secretariat") shall have an Administrative Support Office, Investigation Bureau, Safe Society Division, and Victim Support Monitoring Division.
② One adviser shall be assigned to the chairperson of the Commission (hereinafter referred to as the "chairperson").

Article 4 (Adviser)
① The adviser shall be appointed as a special-service public official equivalent to Grade IV.
② The adviser shall assist the chairperson with the following matters:
1. Research and review of matters concerning the Commission's business as directed by the chairperson;
2. Collection of opinions of organizations representing victims' families concerning the Commission's business.

Article 5 (Administrative Support Office)
① The Administrative Support Office shall have one head and, under that head, one officer each responsible for planning and administration, operational support, and external cooperation.
② The head of the Administrative Support Office shall be appointed from among general-service members of the Senior Executive Service belonging to the Office for Government Policy Coordination or the Ministry of Planning and Budget, and the position shall be classified as Job Grade A. <Amended Dec. 30, 2025>
③ The Planning and Administration Officer shall be appointed from among secretaries belonging to the Office for Government Policy Coordination or the Ministry of Planning and Budget; the Operational Support Officer shall be appointed from among deputy directors general or secretaries; and the External Cooperation Officer shall be a special-service public official equivalent to Grade IV. <Amended Dec. 30, 2025>
④ The Planning and Administration Officer shall assist the head of the Administrative Support Office with the following matters:
1. Comprehensive coordination of the Commission's business;
2. Consultation and coordination concerning ascertainment of the truth of the April 16 Sewol ferry disaster, establishment of comprehensive measures for building a safe society in connection with that disaster, and monitoring of measures to support victims;
3. Convening and operation of meetings of the Commission;
4. Enactment and amendment of rules concerning the operation of the Commission and subcommittees under Article 16 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "subcommittees");
5. Organization and operation of advisory bodies under Article 17 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "advisory bodies");
6. Overall management of the receipt and processing of applications for investigations to ascertain the truth under Article 23 of the Act;
7. Preparation and overall coordination of the comprehensive report under Article 47 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "comprehensive report");
8. Other matters directed by the Secretary-General of the Commission and matters not falling within the jurisdiction of any other division of the Secretariat.
⑤ The Operational Support Officer shall assist the head of the Administrative Support Office with the following matters:
1. General administrative affairs, including management of internal and external events of the Commission;
2. Security, management of official seals, and control of access to Commission premises;
3. Securing and allocation of office space for the Commission;
4. Overall management of the receipt and handling of official documents, postal items, and civil petitions;
5. Establishment, management, and operation of information technology, broadcasting, and communications systems;
6. Appointment, service management, education and training, pensions, and other personnel affairs of staff;
7. Organization and operation of disciplinary committees under Article 20 of the Act;
8. Execution, settlement, and accounting of the Commission's budgets for revenue and expenditure;
9. Payment of salaries and purchase, procurement, and management of goods;
10. Contract administration.
⑥ The External Cooperation Officer shall assist the head of the Administrative Support Office with the following matters:
1. Media relations and media monitoring;
2. Establishment and operation of the Commission's website;
3. Cooperation with the National Assembly, government agencies, and other organizations;
4. Exchanges and cooperation with international investigative institutions and organizations.

Article 6 (Investigation Bureau)
① The Director General of the Investigation Bureau shall be a special-service member of the Senior Executive Service, with the position classified as Job Grade B.
② The Investigation Bureau shall have Investigation Divisions 1, 2, and 3. The head of Investigation Division 1 shall be a prosecutorial investigation secretary, and the heads of Investigation Divisions 2 and 3 shall be special-service public officials equivalent to Grade IV.
③ The head of Investigation Division 1 shall be responsible for the following:
1. Monitoring the progress of activities to ascertain the truth of the April 16 Sewol ferry disaster;
2. Requesting the National Assembly to pass a resolution appointing a special prosecutor in connection with the disaster;
3. Analysis of the results of government investigations into the causes of the disaster;
4. Investigation into the causes of the disaster;
5. Support for the operation of the Truth Investigation Subcommittee under Article 16(1) 1 of the Act;
6. Duties concerning accusations and requests for investigation under Article 28 of the Act, imposition of penalties under Article 51 of the Act (excluding matters falling under Article 7(2) 5 and Article 8(2) 4 of this Decree), accusations under Article 52 of the Act, and imposition and collection of administrative fines under Article 53 of the Act;
7. Conduct of hearings under Article 31 of the Act;
8. Preparation of reports on the results of investigations into matters within its jurisdiction;
9. Preparation of the comprehensive report on matters under the jurisdiction of the Investigation Bureau.
④ The head of Investigation Division 2 shall be responsible for the following:
1. Analysis of government investigation materials concerning rescue and salvage operations related to the April 16 Sewol ferry disaster;
2. Investigation of rescue and salvage operations related to that disaster;
3. Investigation of the appropriateness of the Government's response in connection with the disaster;
4. Preparation of reports on the results of investigations into matters within its jurisdiction.
⑤ The head of Investigation Division 3 shall be responsible for the following:
1. Investigation of the fairness and appropriateness of media reports concerning the April 16 Sewol ferry disaster;
2. Investigation of the defamation of victims through postings on information and communications networks or by other means in connection with that disaster;
3. Collection and preservation of materials concerning the disaster;
4. Preparation of reports on the results of investigations into matters within its jurisdiction.

Article 7 (Safe Society Division)
① The head of the Safe Society Division shall be appointed as a secretary or technical secretary.
② The head of the Safe Society Division shall be responsible for the following:
1. Reforming statutes, systems, policies, practices, and other factors that contributed to the April 16 Sewol ferry disaster and formulating countermeasures;
2. Prevention of calamities and disasters related to the April 16 Sewol ferry disaster;
3. Establishment of comprehensive measures to build a safe society in connection with the disaster;
4. Support for the operation of the Safe Society Subcommittee under Article 16(1) 2 of the Act;
5. Duties concerning imposition of penalties under Article 51 of the Act in matters within the Division's jurisdiction;
6. Preparation of the comprehensive report concerning matters within its jurisdiction.

Article 8 (Victim Support Monitoring Division)
① The head of the Victim Support Monitoring Division shall be appointed as a special-service public official equivalent to Grade IV.
② The head of the Victim Support Monitoring Division shall be responsible for the following:
1. Monitoring measures for supporting victims and bereaved families;
2. Fact-finding surveys concerning support for victims;
3. Support for the operation of the Support Subcommittee under Article 16(1) 3 of the Act;
4. Duties concerning imposition of penalties under Article 51 of the Act in matters within the Division's jurisdiction;
5. Preparation of the comprehensive report concerning matters within its jurisdiction.

Article 9 (Protection of Witnesses and Other Persons)
① The Commission shall not disclose the identity of a person who provides information necessary to ascertain the truth of the April 16 Sewol ferry disaster or submits evidence or materials: Provided, That disclosure may be made with the person's permission.
② Where the Commission recognizes that a commissioner or a witness, reference person, or other person connected with the Commission's duties (hereinafter referred to as a "witness, etc.") has received or is likely to receive threats to their life or body from another person, the Commission may request the relevant agency to provide personal protection for that commissioner or witness, etc. In such cases, the agency receiving the Commission's request shall comply unless there is a justifiable reason not to do so.

Article 10 (Support by Local Governments)
① Where the Commission requests assistance necessary to perform its duties under Article 39 of the Act, a local government shall actively cooperate in the performance of the Commission's duties, including by providing necessary places and personnel.
② Where necessary to decide and coordinate details and methods of cooperation with a local government under paragraph ①, the Commission may jointly establish and operate a working-level consultative council with the relevant local government.
③ The Commission may provide budgetary support to a relevant local government for the processing of duties jointly performed with that local government.

Article 11 (Allowances and Expenses)
Within the limits of the budget, allowances, travel expenses, and other necessary expenses may be paid to commissioners, members of advisory bodies, witnesses, etc. who attend meetings of the Commission or advisory bodies and who are not public officials.

Article 12 (Rules of the Commission)
Matters necessary for the enforcement of this Decree shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the Commission.

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 26235, May 11, 2015>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation.

Article 2 (Transitional Measures Concerning Staff upon Expiration of the Secretariat's Period of Existence)
Where public officials (excluding special-service public officials) are affiliated with the Commission when the Secretariat's period of existence under Article 49 of the Act expires, separate authorized positions corresponding to the number of those officials shall be deemed to exist at the central administrative agencies to which they originally belonged.

ADDENDA (ORGANIZATION OF THE MINISTRY OF THE INTERIOR AND SAFETY AND ITS AFFILIATED AGENCIES) <Presidential Decree No. 28211, July 26, 2017>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation: Provided, That amendments to Presidential Decrees pursuant to Article 8 of these Addenda that amend Presidential Decrees promulgated before this Decree enters into force but whose enforcement dates have not yet arrived shall enter into force on the enforcement dates of the respective Presidential Decrees.

Articles 2 through 7 Omitted.

Article 8 (Amendments to Other Statutes)
(1) through (307) Omitted.
(308) The Enforcement Decree of the Special Act on Ascertaining the Truth of the April 16 Sewol Ferry Disaster and Building a Safer Society is partially amended as follows:

In subparagraph 1 of the Notes to attached Table 1, "Office for Government Policy Coordination; four persons (one secretary or technical secretary, one police senior superintendent, one police superintendent, and one assistant administrative officer for facilities or disaster safety) from the Ministry of Public Safety and Security" is replaced with "Office for Government Policy Coordination";
"five persons (two administrative or information systems officers, one administrative or information systems senior officer or records researcher, and two administrative or information systems officers of the lower grade) from the Ministry of Government Administration and Home Affairs" is replaced with "seven persons (one secretary or technical secretary, two administrative or information systems officers, one administrative or information systems senior officer or records researcher, and three administrative, information systems, facilities, or disaster safety officers of the lower grade) from the Ministry of the Interior and Safety";
and "National Police Agency" is replaced with "National Police Agency; two persons (one police senior superintendent and one police superintendent) from the Korea Coast Guard".

In the Notes to attached Table 2, "Office for Government Policy Coordination; four persons (one secretary or technical secretary, one police senior superintendent, one police superintendent, and one assistant administrative officer for facilities or disaster safety) from the Ministry of Public Safety and Security" is replaced with "Office for Government Policy Coordination";
"five persons (two administrative or information systems officers, one administrative or information systems senior officer or records researcher, and two administrative or information systems officers of the lower grade) from the Ministry of Government Administration and Home Affairs" is replaced with "seven persons (one secretary or technical secretary, two administrative or information systems officers, one administrative or information systems senior officer or records researcher, and three administrative, information systems, facilities, or disaster safety officers of the lower grade) from the Ministry of the Interior and Safety";
and "National Police Agency" is replaced with "National Police Agency; two persons (one police senior superintendent and one police superintendent) from the Korea Coast Guard".

(309) through (388) Omitted.

ADDENDA (ORGANIZATION OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND BUDGET AND ITS AFFILIATED AGENCIES) <Presidential Decree No. 35948, Dec. 30, 2025>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on January 2, 2026: Provided, That amendments made pursuant to Article 4 of these Addenda to Presidential Decrees promulgated before this Decree enters into force but whose enforcement dates have not yet arrived shall enter into force on the enforcement dates of the respective Presidential Decrees.

Articles 2 and 3 Omitted.

Article 4 (Amendments to Other Statutes)
(1) through (135) Omitted.
(136) The Enforcement Decree of the Special Act on Ascertaining the Truth of the April 16 Sewol Ferry Disaster and Building a Safer Society is partially amended as follows:
In Article 5(2) and (3), "Ministry of Strategy and Finance" is replaced, respectively, with "Ministry of Planning and Budget".
(137) through (176) Omitted.
